-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/19198da4-0c51-4d5d-a927-b35e4e25f6d8
-- title:
--   Lemma 18 + divergence decomposition (28): $P_\mu(S)+P_{\hat\mu}(S^c)\ge \frac12\exp(-\sum_{v,j}\mathrm{KL}(\mu_{vj},\hat\mu_{vj})\,\mathbb E_\mu[T_{vj}(t+1)])$ for scheduling histories
-- statement:
--   Fix a scheduling policy $\pi$ for the $U\times K$ switch and two service-probability matrices $\mu,\hat\mu\in[0,1]^{U\times K}$. Assume that every entry where they differ satisfies $0<\hat\mu_{vj}<1$. Let $P_\mu$ and $P_{\hat\mu}$ be the laws of the history $Z^{(t)}$ of the first $t$ slots: the matchings scheduled and the services observed on the scheduled links, generated by $\pi$ from the same i.i.d. uniform seeds under service rates $\mu$ and $\hat\mu$ respectively. Then for every set $S$ of length-$t$ histories,
--   $$P_\mu\big(Z^{(t)}\in S\big)+P_{\hat\mu}\big(Z^{(t)}\notin S\big)\ \ge\ \frac12\exp\Big(-\sum_{v\in[U]}\sum_{j\in[K]}\mathrm{KL}(\mu_{vj},\hat\mu_{vj})\,\mathbb E_\mu[T_{vj}(t+1)]\Big),$$
--   where $\mathrm{KL}(p,q)=p\log\frac pq+(1-p)\log\frac{1-p}{1-q}$ is the Bernoulli divergence and $T_{vj}(t+1)$ is the number of the first $t$ slots in which queue $v$ is served by server $j$.
--
--   This is the step of the proof of Lemma 19 that combines Lemma 18 (Bretagnolle–Huber, $P(\mathcal A)+Q(\mathcal A^c)\ge\frac12e^{-\mathrm{KL}(P\|Q)}$) with the chain-rule identity (28),
--   $$\mathrm{KL}(P_\mu\|P_{\hat\mu})=\sum_{v,j}\mathrm{KL}(\mu_{vj},\hat\mu_{vj})\,\mathbb E_\mu[T_{vj}(t+1)].$$
--   The policy terms of the chain rule cancel because, on a fixed sample path, the policy schedules the same servers under both instances. The paper applies it to its event $\mathcal A=\{T_{uk}(t+1)>t/2\}$, which is a function of the history.
-- source:
--   Krishnasamy, Sen, Johari and Shakkottai, arXiv:1604.06377v4, proof of Lemma 19, pp. 37–39: Lemma 18 (p. 37) applied to P_μ^t, P_μ̂^t, together with the divergence decomposition (28) (p. 39)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_QueueBandit_LateLower_Dynamics

namespace QueueBandit.EarlyLower

/-- Lemma 18 (Bretagnolle–Huber) applied to the laws of the first `t` slots of the scheduling
history under two service-rate matrices, combined with the divergence decomposition (28) of the
proof of Lemma 19 (arXiv:1604.06377v4, pp. 37–39). For every event `S` on histories,
`P_μ(S) + P_μ̂(Sᶜ) ≥ ½ exp(−Σ_{v,j} KL(μ_vj, μ̂_vj) E_μ[T_vj(t+1)])`. Entries where `μ̂` differs
from `μ` must lie in `(0, 1)`, so that the divergence is finite. -/
theorem history_bretagnolle_huber {U K : ℕ} (π : QueueBandit.LateLower.Policy U K)
    (mu muh : Fin U → Fin K → ℝ)
    (hmu : ∀ v j, 0 ≤ mu v j ∧ mu v j ≤ 1) (hmuh : ∀ v j, 0 ≤ muh v j ∧ muh v j ≤ 1)
    (hdiff : ∀ v j, mu v j ≠ muh v j → 0 < muh v j ∧ muh v j < 1)
    (t : ℕ) (S : Set (QueueBandit.LateLower.History U K t)) :
    (1 / 2 : ℝ) * Real.exp (-(∑ v : Fin U, ∑ j : Fin K,
        RegretBandits.Stochastic.klBern (mu v j) (muh v j) *
          QueueBandit.LateLower.expCount π mu v j t)) ≤
      (QueueBandit.LateLower.seedMeasure U K).real
          {ω | QueueBandit.LateLower.hist π mu ω t ∈ S} +
        (QueueBandit.LateLower.seedMeasure U K).real
          {ω | QueueBandit.LateLower.hist π muh ω t ∉ S} := by sorry

end QueueBandit.EarlyLower
